  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2ee87e681215b6147a44021cf30db039, type: 3}
  m_Name: 2.Boat docking
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  nameOfPoint: Boat docking, from Vittoriosa (1867)
  coordinatesPoint: {x: 14.51346, y: 35.89451}
  firstPartInfo: "In December 1867, a small boat landed here, crossing the harbour
    to bring a single prisoner to Valletta for trial - a seventeen-year-old boy called
    Isidoro Mifsud, a baker\u2019s son. His crime was theft - two gold rings and
    twelve Sicilian silver coins that he stole from the home of the Grech family
    in Vittoriosa, with whom he lodged as a domestic servant.  In the boat with him
    was Sergeant Major Giovanni Scicluna, tasked with bringing him to court. Later,
    Scicluna recounted how, during the crossing, Isidoro Mifsud said, \u201C\u2018The
    Devil made me err in judgement; that which I did, I did to aid my brother, who
    I saw yesterday in great need.\u201D [link to doc: Img 1] \u201CMy brother\u201D,
    he said, \u201Cwas in tears because he was so hungry.\u201D [link to doc Img
    2]  This, it seems, was a crime that had its roots in poverty, and the desperation
    it can cause. "
  secondPartInfo: 'From here, Isidoro Mifsud would have been taken up into the city
    - first to the police station, and later to the courts. But before that, his
    steps would take him along a route where the sights of the city impressed themselves
    upon any visitor.  Victoria Gate was still two decades away from being built.
    At the time, there was an older gate, known as the Marina Gate, that granted
    entry to the city from the harbour. There was one path from the harbour up to
    the old gate that is of particular interest to me - a straight and narrow flight
    of steps right between the towering city walls and the backs of the old warehouses.
    That is the path I should take to return to the city. '
  imgMarker: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c98feaebd40d8df449e6d3855ac84352, type: 3}
  imgMain: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f26fa8880b3aeef4b8516c82dc12f2a5, type: 3}
  nameOfStory: Poverty
  hintOfPOI: "Oh yes, those steps\u2026that was one of the main paths that new arrivals
    fresh off a ship would walk to go up into the city. If you\u2019re still standing
    at the quay where the boat carrying Isidoro Muscat docked, you need to walk to
    the left, towards the mouth of the harbour. Keep an eye out for the first flight
    of steps you\u2019ll see on your left. Careful - they\u2019re easy to miss!"
  solutionOfPOI: "It\u2019s a narrow flight of stairs still known as the Nix Mangiare
    steps. You can find them below St. Barbara Bastion, leading from Quarry Wharf
    up to Victoria Gate."
